<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Táchira</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Táchira is a state in the Andes of western Venezuela, on the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombia">Colombia</a>, with its capital at San Cristóbal. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a>-growing region, it produced the Andean leaders who dominated Venezuela from 1899 to 1958, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cipriano-castro">Cipriano Castro</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-vicente-gomez">Juan Vicente Gómez</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleazar-lopez-contreras">Eleazar López Contreras</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaias-medina-angarita">Isaías Medina Angarita</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcos-perez-jimenez">Marcos Pérez Jiménez</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tachira.jpg" alt="Táchira" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Luis Alejandro Escalante Rodríguez · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vistazo_a_la_inmensidad.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Táchira is one of the three Venezuelan Andean states, with Mérida and Trujillo. It covers 11,100 square kilometres and had 1,168,908 inhabitants at the 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. It borders Zulia to the north, Barinas and Mérida to the east, Apure and Barinas to the south, and the Colombian department of Norte de Santander to the west. Its mountains are the southern continuation of the Mérida range, and its highest point, the Páramo El Pulpito, is about 3,995 m above sea level. The Andes of Táchira rose during the Eocene, when the Nazca and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> plates began to slide under the South American plate.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The territory was inhabited by Timote, Cuica and Chibcha groups such as the Machirí, Umuquena and Táriba, and was colonised extensively in the 16th century. Captain Juan Maldonado Ordoñez y Villaquirán founded San Cristóbal in 1561 and Francisco de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caceres">Cáceres</a> founded La Grita in 1576, which began the farming of the area. La Grita was the capital of its province until European trading companies arrived and settled mainly in the more accessible San Cristóbal. The Dominican Friar Alonso de Andrada said the first mass in the territory on 25 July 1558.</p><p>For over two hundred years Táchira was Venezuela's main <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> producer. Important European trading houses arrived in the 19th century, but the region stayed relatively isolated from the rest of the country, and received more cultural influence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombia">Colombia</a>. At the end of the 19th century the national oil industry was born at the place now called La Petrolia, where Venezuela's first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oil-well">oil wells</a> were drilled. Táchira became a state in 1863 and in 1864 joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> of Venezuela as an independent state. In 1881 it joined Guzmán (Mérida) and Trujillo in the Great State of Los Andes, and when that was dissolved in 1899 it recovered its status as a separate state.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The capital, San Cristóbal, lies at 825 metres above sea level on a succession of river terraces and is one of the oldest cities in Venezuela. Its position on the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombia">Colombia</a> makes it a commercial city. Táchira's economy rests on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a>, pineapple, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> and food processing, and the state is connected to Colombia by three international bridges. Its protected areas include El Tamá National Park, Chorro El Indio National Park and the Páramo El Zumbador.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Táchira” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Civil wars and revolts continued until Cipriano Castro and Juan Vicente Gómez, leaders from the Andean state of <b>Táchira</b>, seized Caracas in 1899.</blockquote><a href="/empire/venezuela">Venezuela · Military</a></li></ul></section>
